        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most concrete sealers are dry to the touch in about 1 to 4 hours, ready for foot traffic in roughly 12 to 24 hours and ready for cars in about 48 to 72 hours, while epoxy and urethane coatings often need 5 to 7 days before vehicles. Cool temperatures, high humidity and thick coats can double those times, so treat any chart as a starting point and follow the drying times on your product's label. Dry and cured are also not the same thing. A sealer can feel dry within the hour and still be soft enough for a hot tire to lift it the next afternoon. This guide gives typical times for each sealer type, explains what stretches them, and shows what it means when a sealer stays tacky or turns white.
        </p>

        <div id="chart" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Long Does Each Type of Concrete Sealer Take to Dry?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Concrete sealers fall into three broad families, and they dry very differently. Penetrating sealers, such as silanes, siloxanes and silicates, soak into the concrete and leave little or nothing on the surface, so they tend to be dry to the touch quickly. Acrylic sealers form a thin film on top and come in water-based and solvent-based versions. Epoxy and urethane coatings are thicker two-part or reactive systems that cure chemically and take the longest.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
