        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Most concrete sealers are dry to the touch within a few hours, ready for feet the next day and ready for cars in two to three days. Epoxies and urethanes take closer to a week before vehicles. Those are the typical figures; cool, damp weather and heavy coats stretch them, and the product label is the final word.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Plan the job around the weather rather than around your weekend. A dry slab, a mild morning, thin coats and a 24-hour rain-free forecast prevent nearly every drying problem. Then be patient with the car. The extra day you wait is far cheaper than redoing a driveway with tire-shaped holes in the finish.
          </p>
